WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
If you could talk to your deceased parent, what would you ask them?

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.540
Would you ask them for advice?

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.100
Would you ask them about their childhood?

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.200
Or would it simply be good enough to have the most basic conversation with them?

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.840
One man asks his deceased father questions whenever he wants.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.900
What's more, is he gets answers back.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.520
He programmed a dad bot, a chat bot programmed with his father's recorded answers.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.520
But if you could program an AI version of your parents, would you?

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.840
When James Lajo's father, John, was diagnosed with stage 4 lung cancer, James wanted to

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.560
find a way to keep part of him alive.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.520
In all, they recorded 91,970 words.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.520
He transcribed them into pages that would temporarily sit on a bookshelf.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.780
Then, he had a better idea to keep his dad living beyond this life.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.260
The dad bot.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.740
With his father's approval, James put all those recordings into a software program called

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:01.740
PullString.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.600
To his delight, it allowed for someone without a coding background to program a conversational

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.920
bot.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.380
It works much like Amazon's Alexa and other products with voice recognition and machine

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.720
learning in the conversational computing business.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.940
Dadbot is filled with flaws and doesn't come close to replacing his father, but it was

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.800
never supposed to.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.120
The real magic came from spending time with his father and hearing all the stories he

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.920
would later program into DadBot.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.260
There were concerns that his digital dad would cheapen his relationship with his father or

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.140
that it would be creepy.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.140
But it isn't.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.960
It's just a son trying to connect with his father, even if it's only a trace.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.200
And sometimes, we just want to say, hi dad.
